# VrindaVaani Custom Project Rules

This file documents the strict guidelines, constraints, and professional standards for the VrindaVaani codebase. All AI coding assistants MUST strictly adhere to these rules.

---

## 1. Visual & Graphic Standards
- **Canvas Static Backdrop Fallbacks**: Every theme registered in the settings context must map to a corresponding static/vector drawing routine inside `CelestialParticles.js` to ensure there are no empty/blank backdrops.
- **CPU-Friendly Dynamic Waves**: Any fluid, wave, or particle movement animation drawn in real-time loops must limit canvas recalculations by using coarse step widths (e.g., `x += 15` intervals) instead of pixel-by-pixel increments to maintain high framerates on low-end mobile devices.
- **Rich & Modern Aesthetics**: UIs must feel premium, modern, and trendy. Avoid raw browser defaults or generic colors. Use HSL-tailored palettes, smooth transitions, glassmorphic textures, and subtle micro-animations.
- **Responsive Card Reading Widths**: Always design reading sections with `p-0 bg-transparent border-0` layout on mobile, scaling up to card boxes only on tablet and desktop viewports (`sm:` or `md:`) to maximize readable column width.
- **Avoid Inline Heading Overlaps**: Page headings (`h1`-`h6`) using text-gradients should always be wrapper-isolated using `flex flex-col items-start` to avoid colliding with inline navigation items (`Links`, `buttons`).
- **Active Tab Highlights**: Prefer bottom-border accent lines with smooth transitions (`transition-all duration-300`) over solid capsule container bars for clean modern tab bars.

---

## 2. Performance & App Development
- **Multi-Device Compatibility**: Apps must be fully optimized to run flawlessly from low-end to high-end mobile devices. There must be zero runtime lag, flicker, or visual stuttering.
- **Cache-First (SWR) Architectures**: Implement local caching systems (e.g., synchronous local/context reads combined with asynchronous background revalidation) to ensure instant page navigation and prevent layout shifts/skeleton loader flickers.

---

## 3. Engineering & Clean Builds
- **Zero Warnings Compiler Guarantee**: At the end of every update, verify that the project builds completely clean. There must be **zero** compilation errors, warnings, or ESLint issues.
- **UI/UX Prototyping**: When creating new user interfaces, utilize Stitch tool integrations to design and verify templates before custom styling.
- **Token Efficiency**: Work efficiently and minimize token overhead by avoiding redundant operations or overly verbose subagents.
- **No Unused Variables**: Do not leave unused imports, variables, or timers in React hooks/useEffect cleanups.
- **Zero-Warning Code Quality Enforcement**: Ensure that no unused imports or variables remain in modified files before running build commands, as all ESLint warnings must be pre-resolved in the code rather than ignored.
- **Node/CommonJS Path Isolation**: When executing build-time Node.js helper scripts, always resolve database metadata modules from `/api` or CommonJS targets, to avoid typeless package ES-Module re-parsing overhead.

---

## 4. SEO & Crawlability
- **NoScript Warning Policy**: Never include functional warning phrases (e.g., 'JavaScript required' or 'Please enable JavaScript') in the `<noscript>` tags or pre-rendered headers, to prevent search engines from extracting technical fallbacks as search snippets.

---

## 5. Database & Multi-Repo Schema Synchronization
- **Cross-Component Schema Audits**: When updating database schemas, always perform a search/grep across all linked client applications (e.g. Flutter mobile apps, Next.js web frontends) to ensure database models are fully unified and do not leave out client-required columns.
- **Unified Data Seeding Checklists**: Keep a centralized SQL directory representing the unified database state to make sure schema alterations and seeding are run as cohesive operations across backend and client repos.

- * **PostgreSQL Field Normalization**: When integrating GraphQL-based relational databases (like Firebase SQL Connect or Hasura), always isolate database-to-UI field mapping via a central mapper function (e.g. camelCase to snake_case) to protect client components from backend schema naming drifts.

Config Regex Case-Insensitivity Rule:
Always specify configuration-level regular expressions (such as user-agent matching in routing files) using case-insensitive classes (e.g., [gG]ooglebot) or explicit flags, ensuring case mismatches do not cause silent fallback/routing errors on external crawlers.

Dynamic Route Verification Rule:
When modifying or implementing Server-Side Rendering (SSR) routing handlers, always write and run a localized unit test runner to mock requests (representing both desktop browsers and search engines) to verify that unique titles, meta descriptions, and body payloads are returned before pushing changes.

Accessibility Viewport Compliance Rule:
Never include zoom constraints (maximum-scale or user-scalable=0) in viewport meta tags in any file, including templates, static HTML files, dynamically injected React components, or serverless script handlers.

SPA Server-Side Pre-Rendering Injection Rule:
For Single Page Applications (SPAs) requiring SEO indexation on deep routes, avoid conditional routing based on User-Agent sniffing. Instead, route all page requests to the serverless handler, load the compiled index.html template, inject dynamic metadata/tags, and replace the generic <noscript> section with the page's actual, fully-rendered article content before returning it.

Lambda Static Asset Bundling Rule:
When serverless functions need to read compiled assets (such as index.html or asset-manifest.json) dynamically, always configure Vercel's functions mapping to explicitly bundle those compiled directories (includeFiles: "build/index.html"), ensuring files are resolved during runtime execution.

---

## 6. Free-Tier Serverless & Edge Optimization Rules
- **Aggressive CDN Edge Caching**: Always configure cache headers (`Cache-Control: public, s-maxage=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800`) on all serverless/edge functions that serve dynamic content. This minimizes Edge function invocations and active CPU duration, preventing free-tier limit exhaustions.
- **Static Sitemap Compilation**: Avoid generating XML sitemaps dynamically on every request. Execute sitemap generation once during postbuild compilation to create static files (`sitemap.xml`) served directly from Vercel's CDN, protecting function invocation quotas.
- **Payload Compression & Transfer Optimization**: Keep responses returned by serverless handlers as lightweight as possible. Trim redundant whitespace, compress JSON data, and leverage pagination to minimize Origin Transfer and Data Transfer bandwidth.
- **Local DB Fallbacks & Memory Caching**: Use local JSON files or in-memory caches in API handlers for static/semi-static data (like saints biographies, book metadata) to prevent redundant remote database queries that increase CPU runtimes and database API usage.

optimize_build_times
When building Next.js apps with large datasets (1000+ pages), only pre-render high-value landing pages and SEO hubs in generateStaticParams. Allow long-tail content (e.g., individual verses) to render on-demand at runtime and cache them, saving Vercel build minutes.

Automated Data Integrity Validation:

When making data modifications or database updates, always run a diagnostic validation script to verify that no numeric slugs, empty titles, or duplicate primary keys are created, ensuring data stays 100% clean before committing files.

Incremental Validation Builds:

When updating content data or layout configurations, always trigger an offline compilation check to confirm that no new dynamic routes trigger compilation warnings or break static page pre-rendering (SSG).

# Recommended Rules

1. [performance] Whenever performing heavy remote API fetches or database queries (such as Firebase Data Connect, Supabase, or external REST endpoints) during Next.js Server Side Rendering (SSR/SSG), always implement local filesystem-based caching (with configurable TTLs in development and persistent build-time assets in production) to keep build times under 1 minute and prevent Vercel execution timeouts.
2. [compiler-cleanliness] If Next.js prerendering or build-phase compilation issues (e.g., "Cannot find module './chunks/...'") occur due to state drift, always delete the `.next` compiler directory cache (`rm -rf .next`) before attempting a rebuild.
3. [slug-limits] Cap dynamic route slugs and local filename generations to 100 characters max and automatically sanitize Devanagari/UTF-8 strings into ASCII/Hinglish to prevent macOS/Linux filesystem ENAMETOOLONG errors.

Keep build caches and serialized caches synchronized by automatically checking for shape/property changes when parsing pre-built JSON cache layers.

Define cache thresholds (e.g., IndexedDB or lightweight gzip backups) whenever bundle or assets size on client-side routing exceeds 15MB, ensuring low-end mobile devices can parse local cache instantly.

When migrating client-side data providers, always ensure the client-side cache miss fallback matches the new server-side provider syntax, using direct SDK fetches over the network instead of stale RTDB sync blocks.

- Cache large datasets flat on disk; avoid serializing nested relationship maps to prevent file footprint explosion.
- Reconstruct relational links in-memory dynamically on start (in-memory parsing is always faster than reading/parsing duplicated JSON objects).
- Avoid synchronous disk writes (`fs.writeFileSync`) in route initialization or component render pathways; always prefer async `fs.writeFile` to keep the Event Loop non-blocking.

- For every dynamic route directory (especially database-linked routes), always create a sibling loading.js file containing an instantly-rendering skeleton loader to eliminate page navigation transition lag.

When implementing skeleton loaders in Next.js, always align the structure, layouts, spacing, and grids of the loading placeholders (loading.js) with their target page components (page.js) to provide a seamless visual transition on client-side routing.

Always process batched API calls (e.g. LLM inferences, vector embeddings, external network lookups) using a concurrency-controlled parallel execution pool (pMap) instead of sequential loops, ensuring maximum throughput without hitting rate limits.

To avoid navigation lag on client-side routing, intercept Link onClick events in custom router shims to dispatch instant transition events and swap the active layout with a matching PageSkeleton immediately, running dynamic page fetching in the background.

Rule for Next.js Server/Client Boundaries: Always separate raw static data structures (like FAQ arrays, page list configurations, or metadata arrays) from Client Components (files marked with 'use client';). Place them in dedicated data files (e.g., in a src/data/ folder) to prevent Next.js from proxying them as client boundaries, which causes runtime and build-time prerendering errors when imported by Server Components.

Rule for Author Page and E-E-A-T Signals: Every content writer, editor, or reviewer mentioned in our article schemas must have a corresponding profile entry on the /author page detailing their qualifications, academic credentials, and manuscript/study background in Sanskrit/Braj literature to establish authority and trust with search engines.

Rule for Schema Validation: Whenever updating schema generators (in seoSchemas.js) or adding new JSON-LD metadata fields to routes, run a local compile test (npm run build) and cross-check the generated HTML tags against Google Rich Result schema specs to ensure sitemap configurations do not throw warnings.

[perf_cooperative_rendering]: "Always execute large-dataset modifications (e.g. array filtering, text transliteration, database parses above 1MB) asynchronously or chunked inside requestIdleCallback or deferred timers to maintain high responsiveness and prevent frame drops (jank) in mobile environments."
[perf_state_minimization]: "Never initialize React state synchronously with heavy operations (like reading local storage or parsing complex JSON files) unless they are tiny (<10KB) or strictly required for initial render. Wrap fallback reads inside initialization functions or fetch asynchronously after mount."

Rule [performance_frame_budget]:
Defer heavy parsing operations, secondary React effect checks, and offscreen asset loading using requestIdleCallback or progressive preloading lists, keeping initial JS execution under 100ms on low-performance cores.

Rule [layout_stability_cls]:
Ensure all static elements (headers, sidebars, hero grids) have fixed heights or predefined aspect ratios so that loading content or mounting components does not cause Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) above 0.1.

Rule [accessible_navigation_a11y]:
Equip all touch controls and action buttons (e.g., chanting bead counter, theme buttons, profile avatar links) with clear aria-label tags and visible interactive states for screen readers and keyboard access.

Rule for Database File Tracing:
When introducing or modifying any client-side/server-side dynamic page that reads local JSON/cache files at runtime on Next.js/Vercel, always ensure the read target is added to the outputFileTracingIncludes configuration in next.config.mjs to prevent runtime 404 errors.

Rule for Copyright Auditing:
When importing external content databases, always strip all modern translations, commentaries, and metadata beforehand to avoid DMCA takedown alerts, keeping only public domain texts and generating transliterations programmatically.

Always Verify Builds Post-Refactor:

When modifying layout components, styling classes, or imports, always run a production-equivalent build (npm run build or framework equivalent) synchronously or in the background to guarantee zero regressions or TypeScript/lint compilation errors.

Clean Up Unused Components & Imports:

When deprecating or disabling a UI element or feature, do not leave unused imports or dead React component instances rendered in parent templates. Ensure both the import statements and JSX tags are fully pruned.

- **SEO Slug Protection**: Never overwrite or regenerate database `slug` fields during sanitization or database updates. Always preserve the original indexed URLs (derived from sitemaps or original sources) to prevent breaking Google indexing and causing 404 errors.

